\title{Layer Selection Sensitivity in Hidden-State Safety Probes for Reasoning Models}

\author{Anonymous ARR Submission}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\input{sections/abstract}
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/discussion}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\input{sections/limitations}

\bibliography{references}

\input{sections/appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought can transition from safety-aware deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input modification~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}.

\textit{Attack characterization.} Self-jailbreak has been documented with step-level annotations~\cite{harmthoughts}, with longer chains increasing circumvention probability~\cite{selfjailbreak}. Reasoning models can autonomously jailbreak other LLMs with 97\% success~\cite{hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak}, and can be manipulated through self-generated reasoning artifacts~\cite{xu2025manipulated}. H-CoT~\cite{hcot2025} shows reasoning chains can be externally hijacked. Unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful} further motivates hidden-state monitoring that bypasses text. Safety-aligned models maintain refusal intentions during thinking but experience a sharp drop at output generation~\cite{yin2025refusal_cliff}, a phenomenon our temporal probes target. Refusal dynamics in standard LLMs~\cite{hu2026refusal_dynamics} and information leakage in traces~\cite{ahrend2026safer_traces} provide additional context.

\textit{Detection.} CoT monitoring can detect reward hacking but optimization pressure promotes obfuscation~\cite{baker2025monitoring}. CoT monitorability is valuable but fragile~\cite{korbak2025cot_monitorability}. GuardReasoner~\cite{xiang2025guardreasoner} proposes a dedicated lightweight guard model; \citet{su2025reasoning_tokens} unify reasoning tokens and safety in a single framework.

\textit{Mitigation.} SafeThink~\cite{safethink} steers early reasoning toward safety, SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered reminders, and Chain-of-Guardrails~\cite{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning} proposes guardrail insertion during reasoning. None of these conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations can detect safety violations in standard LLMs. \citet{zhou2024alignment} show safety-relevant information distributes across layers, while \citet{qi2025shallow_alignment} find that alignment operates primarily over early output tokens. \citet{arditi2024refusal} identify a single refusal direction, but \citet{zhao2025harmfulness} show harmfulness and refusal are encoded separately. \citet{joad2026refusal_multi} reveal eleven category-specific refusal directions, and \citet{wollschlager2025geometry} formalize multi-dimensionality through concept cones, consistent with our finding that single-direction methods are insufficient. TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance from safe trajectories; SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers; SIREN~\cite{siren} monitors internal representations during generation; SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026} proposes proactive world models for detection. These methods assume threats from external input manipulation and operate at the token level on instruction-tuned models. The self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. \citet{kadali2025internal} decompose internal layer representations to separate jailbreak from benign prompts; their results confirm that layer-wise structure carries safety-relevant signal. We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails because discriminative signal resides in minor PCA components.

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Linear probing has a long history from layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding} through sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing} to comprehensive surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing}. \citet{hewitt2019control} introduce control tasks to distinguish structure from memorization, which we adopt (\cref{app:control}). \citet{belrose2023leace} establish theoretical limits on linear probe extraction; \citet{elazar2021amnesic} test whether probed information is causally used. \citet{tenney2019bert} show syntactic information in middle layers and semantics in deeper layers. \citet{zhang2025probing_selfverify} apply hidden-state probing to self-verification in reasoning models, a complementary perspective on what reasoning-model representations encode. Concurrent work by \citet{alvarez2026transport_geometry} uses hidden-state geometry for step-level hallucination detection; the approach shares our temporal probing framework but targets a different phenomenon. \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show that linear probes can rely on textual evidence rather than deeper representations, which motivates our comparison of hidden-state probes against text-based monitors. Despite this foundation, layer selection remains underexamined: most studies select by a single metric without sensitivity analysis. We demonstrate that this practice can reverse a study's central conclusion (\cref{sec:layer-results}).

\paragraph{Change-Point Detection.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions via consistency constraints; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions through mean differences. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}). BOCPD and CUSUM fail because commitment is gradual. CCS provides no gain because its consistency objective misaligns with temporal structure. RepE shows inconsistent performance across models (0.756 on QwQ, 0.559 on R1-32B). These failures motivate supervised probes with principled layer selection as the minimal effective methodology.

